sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, ADR 0241 allowlists, DECISIONS R1-R4 + 5 (process.stop shape, shared SSH identity).
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-03, rule 22 (no script: one ufw argv per change over @swamp/ssh exec; a typed step plans positions, Lab #4334).
  %% Decided by Nick, 2026-11-07 (shape spike): the whole Node side is one Ansible phase; the plan is a Jinja template with Molecule unit cases, applied with the `community.general.ufw` module (it can insert at a row and name a port range).
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: firewall:allow
  %% kind: firewall.allow
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: firewall:allow PORT<br/>--node=NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit firewall:allow PORT --node=NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the destination port or port range
  %%> - the Node, and optionally the source address and the protocol
  %%> - optionally a label or short description
  %%> Not accepted:
  %%> - service names such as `postgres` as the port
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, record the rule
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node
  %%> - the target is an active Linux Node
  %%> - the port and source are valid
  %%> - no opposite rule for the same port, protocol and source; an allow and a deny for exactly the same scope is refused (`firewall.rule_conflict`)
  %%> - no other firewall change runs on the target; the Gateway holds one lock per Node
  %%> Allowed:
  %%> - repeat an active or failed allow rule: reuse its record and check the Node in a new task
  %%> - overlapping rules with a different scope, such as a broad deny with an allow for one source
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the new or reused rule is recorded with status provisioning
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent
  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-firewall-ansible
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the rule as exact ufw lines: port, protocol, source, direction
  %%> - the target's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task ID, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Converge the rule<br/>one Ansible phase · ufw
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one Ansible phase on the target, over SSH from the Swamp Node
  %%> - reads `ufw status numbered` and refuses an inactive firewall (`firewall.backend_inactive`) before any change
  %%> - plans the lines this rule needs, in order, and applies them one `ufw` module call at a time
  %%> - reads `ufw status numbered` again and checks that every expected line is present, in narrow-first order
  %%> - writes the public result (`active`, or the skip reason, or the error code) for the Gateway
  %%> Order:
  %%> - narrower rules go above broader ones, allow or deny, so an exception such as one IP wins over a rule for anywhere
  %%> - ufw stops at the first match, so Orbit sets this order on every change
  %%> - all operator rules sit above Orbit's own broad rules, such as WireGuard peer trust, so a deny on a port also applies to WireGuard peers
  %%> Already applied:
  %%> - plans no changes when every expected line already exists in the correct position
  %%> - the check still runs before the task can succeed, and the result is `Skipped — already applied (firewall.rule_exists)`
  %%> A line in the wrong place:
  %%> - is deleted and inserted again at the right row
  %%> Why one phase:
  %%> - the read, the plan, each change and the check are tasks of one playbook, not Swamp steps: a rerun changes nothing, and the Swamp run is three steps (input, phase, result)
  alt changes needed
    %% path: changes-needed
    Note over N: Change the rules<br/>ufw module · one call per line
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - adds one ufw allow line per port, protocol and family, commented with its label
    %%> - inserts each line at the row the plan names; an existing identical, well-placed line is left as it is
    %%> - moves a line that sits in the wrong place: deletes it, then inserts it again
    %%> - nothing is copied to the Node
  else already applied
    %% path: already-applied
    Note over SW: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the plan contains no changes
    %%> - the check still runs before the task can succeed
  end
  alt rules present and ordered
    %% path: succeeded
    N-->>SW: ufw lines in order
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed for every line and its narrow-first order
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the rule becomes active
    %%> - the task succeeds
    %%> - if no changes were needed, reports `Skipped — already applied (firewall.rule_exists)`
    %%> - the skip reason is part of the successful result in human and machine output, not an error
  else adding or checking fails
    %% path: failed
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0
    %%> When:
    %%> - ufw refuses the line, SSH fails, or a line is missing after the add
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark the rule failed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the rule is marked failed, so you can see it
    %%> - the task fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - lines added before the failure stay; there is no rollback
    %%> - that is safe: the rule is marked failed, and a rerun skips lines that already exist
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
